WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.686
Military procurement failures.

00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.204
Decades late, billions over.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:11.284
Canada spends thirty-five point seven billion dollars annually on defence but cannot equip its military.

00:00:11.284 --> 00:00:18.215
The surface combatant program to replace the Halifax-class frigates has experienced repeated delays and cost overruns.

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:25.431
The CF-18 fighter fleet is decades past its intended service life — the replacement process took over thirty years.

00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:29.897
The Victoria-class submarines have spent more time in maintenance than at sea.

00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:35.477
Each procurement failure is documented by the Auditor General and the Parliamentary Budget Officer.

00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:44.181
Each failure leaves the Canadian Armed Forces less capable of meeting its NATO commitments while defence contractors profit from extended timelines and change orders.

00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:49.999
Comparison table showing major procurement programs, their original timelines, and actual delivery.

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:55.022
Three major procurement failures documented from AG and PBO reports.

00:00:55.022 --> 00:00:57.215
The procurement capture loop.

00:00:57.215 --> 00:01:01.829
Defence procurement failures serve institutional interests at every stage.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:06.568
Defence contractors profit from delays through change orders and extended contracts.

00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:11.795
The lobbying industrial complex ensures procurement decisions favour connected contractors.

00:01:11.795 --> 00:01:18.102
Politicians announce programs for political benefit while delivery timelines extend beyond their electoral cycles.

00:01:18.102 --> 00:01:25.954
The military leadership that would have demanded accountability was removed during the CDS turnover documented in the military purge analysis.

00:01:25.954 --> 00:01:35.340
And the procurement process itself — managed through PSPC rather than DND — creates bureaucratic layers that slow delivery while increasing administrative costs.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:43.193
The result: thirty-five point seven billion dollars annually producing declining capability while defence contractors report healthy profits.
